---
/**
 * p67 · Filed
 *
 * Plozz keeps a wall of covers, and a cover is the flattest thing there is: a
 * rectangle standing in for something you have not watched yet. These four
 * marks are four ways of stopping it being flat.
 *
 * A library is not one cover. It is this cover and the one behind it, and
 * the reason you can tell them apart is that they are lit by the same light
 * from slightly different distances. So the transformation here is the
 * smallest one that turns a tile into an object: put a second tile behind it.
 *
 * The back cover is shaded against its own whole rectangle rather than
 * against the sliver of it you can see, which is what stops it reading as a
 * rim highlight welded to the front one. Its bevel runs the same way, one
 * step deeper, because it is further from the light.
 *
 * The seam between them is a single pixel of ink, not two. Every pixel that
 * meets the outside is inked, and so is every pixel that meets something
 * behind it — one rule, and the result is that the front cover is outlined
 * over the back one without the two outlines doubling up.
 *
 * All four are lit by one law, so they read as a family rather than as four
 * ideas. Depth inside the silhouette is measured as a blurred signed distance
 * — blurred because the raw nearest-outside vector flips between straight and
 * diagonal at every step of a stepped edge, and quantising that puts notches
 * in a shape that has none. Tone is then `5 + 5 · lambert · shell(depth)`,
 * where `shell` reaches zero at seven pixels in. Beyond that the field is a
 * single flat tone: the centre is where the face lives and nothing competes
 * with it. The light is upper left in every one of them.
 *
 * The normal is bent outward by the body's own curvature as well as by the
 * nearest edge, so the bands taper along an edge instead of tracking it at a
 * constant offset. A constant offset is a closed loop, and a closed loop reads
 * as a frame drawn around a plaque rather than as light falling on a solid.
 *
 * 11 tones and one ink. The face is `md` with the `compact` smile,
 * sitting entirely on the flat centre — nothing is cleared for it.
 */
import MarkFrame from '../MarkFrame.astro';
import { facePathsAt } from '../../../data/mark';

interface Props { size?: number }
const { size = 128 } = Astro.props;
---

<MarkFrame size={size} title="Plozz — Filed">
